# quality-mgr note, 2026-09-05, re: fix/aw-readiness-api round-2 QA (qa-pr1211-r2):
# the real fix for this finding (CASE-based severity rank replacing the lexical
# compare) lands on the sibling branch fix/aw-readiness-easy (PR #1215), not on
# fix/aw-readiness-api (PR #1211). arch-qa/ruthless-boundary-qa confirmed it closed
# there. fix/aw-readiness-api adds only a client-side re-filter workaround in
# crates/atm/src/commands/log.rs (comment: "not level-order-correct, tracked
# separately") pending that merge. Status stays "open" here until PR #1215 merges
# forward into this lineage and the workaround in log.rs is removed as dead code.

# quality-mgr, 2026-09-06, qa-phase-aw-integration-review-r2: CORRECTING an
# earlier (incorrect) internal assessment that treated this record as closed.
# It is NOT closed. ruthless-boundary-qa's fresh review at
# integrate/phase-aw@062cb44dd, cross-checked directly by me against
# crates/atm/src/commands/log.rs:222-235, confirms: the server-side query fix
# (CASE-based severity rank, crates/atm-storage-rusqlite/src/
# diagnostic_timeline.rs:133-138) IS present and correct -- so any direct
# HTTP consumer of /v1/diagnostics now gets correct filtering. But this
# record's own recorded closure condition ("status stays open until PR #1215
# merges forward into this lineage and the workaround in log.rs is removed as
# dead code") is NOT satisfied: log.rs:222-235 still hard-codes
# `level_at_least: None` with a comment claiming the server-side filter "is a
# lexicographic string comparison and is not level-order-correct" -- now
# false and stale -- and the CLI still maintains a second, independent
# client-side severity filter (CliLogLevel/LogLevelFilter, log.rs:76-105)
# duplicating the decision the server now makes correctly. Two parallel
# severity-filtering implementations that can silently drift again. Remains
# genuinely open. Flagging to fenix/team-lead for a fix dispatch or an
# explicit deferral ruling; not something quality-mgr can close unilaterally.

# Persisted file/path values are repository-relative. Runtime checkout paths
# belong in the agent input only and must not be copied into this record.

<urn:atm:triage:finding/AW-READY-M7>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "AW-READY-M7" ;
  triage:title "/v1/diagnostics?level= performs a lexical string compare instead of a severity-rank comparison, so level=info silently drops all error rows" ;
  triage:description "fenix reviewer-A (01M1S6XV64XHNGSP00NWXK26RQ) and fenix-C (01M1S751VVM0K4C7ZW6YDPC7BQ, their F1, duplicate noted as O1, counted once here); quality-mgr confirmed the exact query text: crates/atm-storage-rusqlite/src/diagnostic_timeline.rs:92 query against TEXT column storing lowercase level-name strings (error/warn/info/trace). Lexical ordering puts error below info, so level=info silently drops every error row instead of including it. No test exercises level_at_least semantics. The CLI itself is unaffected because it filters client-side after fetching (crates/atm/src/commands/log.rs:232-238), but any direct HTTP consumer of the route is exposed. Fix: integer rank column or CASE expression for severity ordering; test error inclusion at warn/info thresholds." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-aw" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "query-correctness" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:repeatable true ;
  triage:sweepScope "workspace" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-09-05T16:52:43Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:foundIn triage:AW3 ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-09-05T16:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AW-READY-M7/AW3-query-1> ;
  triage:triageRecordVersion "1" .

